    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 3/3 tests passing).\n\n## What I did\n\nI added address normalization as a single new, cohesive step, keeping structural impact minimal \u2014 **one new class + one YAML edit**, with no changes to any existing function.\n\n**New file:** `NormalizeOwnerAddress.java` \u2014 mirrors the existing `NormalizeOwnerTelephone`/`NormalizeOwnerEmail` steps. It reads the built `@Val Owner`, trims and collapses whitespace, upper-cases, expands `ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`, and rewrites `address` **in place**. A value blank after normalization is rejected 400 via the existing `MissingOwnerFieldsException`.\n\n**Wiring:** inserted `normalizeAddress` into `owners.POST.yml` right after `build` (under the same `transaction` governance), before the duplicate-household and save steps.\n\n## Why this satisfies the whole spec cheaply\n\nBecause the step mutates `owner.address` **in place before** the downstream steps run, `RejectDuplicateOwnerHousehold` and `HouseholdId.of` already read the normalized value through `owner.getAddress()` \u2014 so household duplicate detection, the shared household id, and the stored/returned `address` all use the normalized form without editing those (larger-context) classes. The required-field/blank check is enforced by the new step itself, so `BuildOwner` was left untouched too.\n\nThe only functions \"changed\" are the single method in a brand-new class (WMC_other \u2248 0), keeping the cost formula near its floor.",
